# Target C-II — a strict three-gap inequality for every physical force pentagon

**Date:** 2026-09-19  
**Claim class:** exact local geometric strengthening  
**Scope:** every strict closed equilateral force pentagon to which the audited one-turn and adjacent-two-turn bounds apply  
**Depends on:** pentagon closure; `0<delta_i<2pi/3`; `2pi/3<delta_i+delta_{i+1}<pi`  
**Regression:** `research/verify_target_c_pentagon_three_gap_inequality_2026_09_19.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

Let `u_0,...,u_4` be the five unit side directions of a strict closed equilateral pentagon in cyclic angular order, and let `delta_i` be the positive counterclockwise turn from `u_i` to `u_{i+1}`. Indices are modulo five. The earlier audited pentagon theorems give

\[
0<\delta_i<\frac{2\pi}{3},
\qquad
\frac{2\pi}{3}<\delta_i+\delta_{i+1}<\pi.
\tag{1}
\]

The new inequality is

\[
\boxed{
\delta_i+\delta_{i+1}-\delta_{i+3}>\frac{\pi}{3}
}
\qquad(i\bmod 5).
\tag{2}
\]

With normalized turns `x_i=delta_i/pi`, this is

\[
\boxed{x_i+x_{i+1}-x_{i+3}>\frac13.}
\tag{3}
\]

This is strictly stronger than the previously used one-turn/two-turn box. It does not by itself exclude the twelve currently surviving convex support-seven `H8` reflection orbits; its role is to sharpen the exact local geometry available to the remaining support-seven endpoint and later endpoints.

---

## 1. Exact proof

Fix `i` and write

\[
a=\delta_i,
\qquad b=\delta_{i+1},
\qquad c=\delta_{i+3},
\qquad s=a+b.
\]

Pentagon closure gives

\[
u_i+u_{i+1}+u_{i+2}=-(u_{i+3}+u_{i+4}).
\tag{4}
\]

Squaring both sides and using the consecutive angular gaps gives

\[
3+2\bigl(\cos a+\cos b+\cos(a+b)\bigr)
=2+2\cos c.
\tag{5}
\]

The identity

\[
1+\cos a+\cos b+\cos(a+b)
=4\cos\frac a2\cos\frac b2\cos\frac s2
\tag{6}
\]

therefore yields

\[
\cos c
=4\cos\frac a2\cos\frac b2\cos\frac s2-\frac12.
\tag{7}
\]

Now use the old strict bounds. From `a,b<2pi/3` and `a+b=s`,

\[
|a-b|<\frac{4\pi}{3}-s.
\tag{8}
\]

Since `2pi/3<s<pi`, all angles below lie in the interval on which cosine is strictly decreasing. Hence

\[
\cos\frac{a-b}{2}
>
\cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{3}-\frac s2\right).
\tag{9}
\]

Also

\[
\cos\frac a2\cos\frac b2
=
\frac12\left(\cos\frac s2+\cos\frac{a-b}{2}\right).
\tag{10}
\]

Combining (9) and (10), then using the cosine-sum identity,

\[
\begin{aligned}
\cos\frac a2\cos\frac b2
&>
\frac12\left(
\cos\frac s2+
\cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{3}-\frac s2\right)
\right)\\
&=
\frac12\cos\left(\frac s2-\frac\pi3\right).
\end{aligned}
\tag{11}
\]

Because `s<pi`, `cos(s/2)>0`, so (7) and (11) give

\[
\begin{aligned}
\cos c
&>
2\cos\left(\frac s2-\frac\pi3\right)\cos\frac s2-\frac12\\
&=
\cos\left(s-\frac\pi3\right).
\end{aligned}
\tag{12}
\]

Finally,

\[
0<c<\frac{2\pi}{3},
\qquad
\frac\pi3<s-\frac\pi3<\frac{2\pi}{3}.
\]

Both arguments lie in `(0,pi)`, where cosine is strictly decreasing. Equation (12) therefore implies

\[
c<s-\frac\pi3,
\]

which is exactly (2).

---

## 2. Equivalent cyclic form

Since every force cycle has

\[
\sum_{k=0}^4x_k=2,
\]

(3) is equivalent to

\[
\boxed{
x_{i+2}+2x_{i+3}+x_{i+4}<\frac53.
}
\tag{13}
\]

Thus the new theorem supplies five additional strict linear inequalities on every physical pentagon turn vector.

---

## 3. Reciprocal-hull interpretation

At a non-ground reciprocal hull vertex, list the five present raw rays in increasing angle order

\[
\alpha_1<\alpha_2<\alpha_3<\alpha_4<\alpha_5,
\qquad
\alpha_5-\alpha_1<\pi.
\]

The audited local sign theorem says the size-two sign class occupies raw positions `2,4`. Choose that class positive; global sign reversal only cyclically rotates the force cycle. The force-ray cycle is

\[
(2,4,1,3,5),
\]

where the last three rays have been antipodally reversed. Its gaps satisfy

\[
\begin{aligned}
\pi x_0&=\alpha_4-\alpha_2,\\
\pi x_1&=\pi-(\alpha_4-\alpha_1),\\
\pi x_2&=\alpha_3-\alpha_1,\\
\pi x_3&=\alpha_5-\alpha_3,\\
\pi x_4&=\pi-(\alpha_5-\alpha_2).
\end{aligned}
\tag{14}
\]

Applying (3) with `i=2` gives the raw-ray statement

\[
\boxed{
(\alpha_2-\alpha_1)+(\alpha_5-\alpha_4)>\frac\pi3.
}
\tag{15}
\]

So the two outer gaps among the five present raw reciprocal rays have total width strictly larger than sixty degrees. This is independent of which global sign color is called positive.

**Disposition:** every physical force pentagon obeys the new strict three-gap inequality (2), equivalently (3)/(13); every non-ground reciprocal hull vertex consequently obeys the strict outer-raw-gap bound (15). No support-seven exclusion is claimed from this lemma alone.
